q('An adult with an unknown tetanus-vaccination history has a dirty puncture wound. Which preventive strategy is appropriate?', 'Wound care, age-appropriate tetanus-containing vaccine and tetanus immune globulin',[
 ('Antibiotics alone as a substitute for vaccine and TIG','They are not the recommended tetanus prophylaxis.'),('Vaccine alone always suffices with unknown primary-series status and a dirty wound','Passive protection is also indicated.'),('TIG alone as permanent active immunity','It does not establish lasting vaccine immunity.')],
 'Wound type and primary-series status determine tetanus prevention', '**Dirty major wounds and incomplete/unknown vaccination can require both active and passive protection.**',['Clean the wound and remove devitalized material as indicated.','Give vaccine and TIG using the correct separate-site technique.'], '**Arrange completion of the primary series.**',['Previously completed series have different booster rules.','Treat a bacterial wound infection when present, but not as a substitute for tetanus protection.'],[['Wound','Dirty puncture'],['History','Unknown/incomplete'],['Prevention','Vaccine plus TIG and wound care']],[TE],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Is tetanus immune globulin indicated for a clean minor wound solely because vaccination history is unknown?', 'No; provide indicated tetanus vaccine and wound care, but TIG is not used for clean minor wounds',[
 ('TIG is mandatory for every wound regardless of type','The wound classification matters.'),('Unknown history means vaccine should also be withheld','Active vaccination is indicated.'),('A topical antibiotic provides lifelong tetanus immunity','It does not.')],
 'Passive-antibody indications are narrower than vaccine indications', '**CDC does not recommend TIG for clean minor wounds.**',['Unknown or incomplete vaccine history still needs an active-immunization plan.','Dirty or major wounds use a different algorithm.'], '**Document wound category and vaccination history.**',['Schedule remaining doses when needed.','Do not use antibiotics specifically as tetanus prophylaxis.'],[['Clean minor','No TIG'],['Unknown series','Vaccinate'],['Dirty/major','Assess the separate TIG pathway']],[TE])
q('Which prevention principle is central for an adult after splenectomy?', 'Review the indicated pneumococcal, meningococcal and other national asplenia vaccines, together with an emergency fever plan',[
 ('Vaccination removes every need to act on fever','Overwhelming infection can still occur.'),('Only a tetanus booster is relevant to asplenia','Encapsulated-bacterial risks require broader planning.'),('The same unreviewed schedule applies regardless of prior doses or jurisdiction','Recommendations and prior history affect the plan.')],
 'Asplenia prevention combines immunization and rapid action', '**Pneumococcal and meningococcal prevention is particularly important.**',['Hib recommendations depend on prior vaccination and the national pathway.','Influenza and routine vaccines also matter.'], '**Provide clear written fever and antibiotic instructions according to local policy.**',['Vaccines reduce but do not eliminate risk.','Review boosters, travel and relevant exposure risks.'],[['Vaccines','Encapsulated-pathogen and routine protection'],['Emergency','Prompt fever assessment'],['Follow-up','Schedule/booster review']],[SP])
q('A person with HIV has CD4 90 cells/mm3. Which routine live-vaccine principle is appropriate?', 'Avoid MMR and live varicella vaccine during this severe immunodeficiency and plan reassessment after immune recovery',[
 ('All live vaccines are safe whenever HIV RNA is low, regardless of CD4','Cellular immune status remains important.'),('Every non-live vaccine is contraindicated as well','Non-live vaccines generally have different safety considerations.'),('No vaccines should ever be offered again after HIV diagnosis','Effective ART can restore eligibility for selected live vaccines.')],
 'Live-vaccine safety depends on the degree of immune impairment', '**CD4 below 200 is an important restriction for MMR/live varicella in adults with HIV.**',['Other vaccine recommendations depend on immune status and product.','Inactivated and recombinant vaccines can remain indicated.'], '**Use the HIV-specific immunization schedule.**',['Review immunity and prior doses.','Reassess after immune recovery without delaying urgently indicated non-live protection.'],[['Severe immunodeficiency','Avoid specified live vaccines'],['Non-live products','Different safety pathway'],['Later','Reassess with immune recovery']],[HI],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why can recombinant zoster vaccine be offered to eligible immunocompromised adults?', 'It contains no live varicella-zoster virus and is designed to prevent zoster in the eligible risk population',[
 ('It is the same product as live varicella vaccine','The products and purposes differ.'),('It treats active shingles in place of antiviral therapy','It is a preventive vaccine.'),('It replaces varicella-exposure management in a susceptible person','Primary-varicella exposure requires a different pathway.')],
 'Vaccine platform and indication must both be understood', '**Recombinant zoster vaccine is non-live.**',['CDC recommends it for eligible immunocompromised adults using a two-dose schedule.','Timing around immune-suppressive therapy can influence response.'], '**Do not confuse zoster prevention with primary varicella prevention or treatment.**',['Assess prior varicella history according to guidance.','Plan the best feasible vaccination timing.'],[['Platform','Recombinant, non-live'],['Purpose','Prevent zoster'],['Not a substitute','Acute treatment or primary-varicella PEP']],[RZ])
q('A pregnant patient lacks rubella immunity. What is the routine MMR approach?', 'Defer MMR during pregnancy and arrange postpartum vaccination',[
 ('Give MMR immediately in every trimester','It is a live vaccine contraindicated during pregnancy.'),('Never vaccinate after pregnancy because nonimmunity persists','Postpartum vaccination protects future pregnancies.'),('Treat rubella nonimmunity with antibiotics','Antibiotics do not create rubella immunity.')],
 'A live-vaccine contraindication can be temporary', '**MMR is avoided during known pregnancy.**',['Document nonimmunity and the postpartum plan.','Inadvertent administration requires counseling rather than unsupported conclusions.'], '**Complete prevention after delivery.**',['Use the recommended interval advice before a future pregnancy.','Review other indicated maternal and postpartum vaccines.'],[['Pregnancy','No MMR'],['Postpartum','Vaccinate when indicated'],['Goal','Protect future pregnancies']],[PG],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which influenza-vaccine principle is appropriate during pregnancy?', 'An indicated inactivated or recombinant influenza vaccine can be given; live intranasal influenza vaccine is avoided',[
 ('Every influenza vaccine is contraindicated throughout pregnancy','That is incorrect.'),('Only the live nasal product is appropriate','Live attenuated vaccine is avoided.'),('Vaccination must always wait until after delivery regardless of season or risk','Pregnancy is a relevant prevention setting.')],
 'Vaccine formulation changes pregnancy suitability', '**Inactivated influenza vaccination is recommended in pregnancy according to the current schedule.**',['It protects a higher-risk patient group.','The live attenuated intranasal product has different restrictions.'], '**Use the available appropriate formulation and timing.**',['Review allergy and prior reactions.','Do not delay a suitable vaccine solely because it is early in pregnancy.'],[['Inactivated/recombinant','Appropriate when indicated'],['Live intranasal','Avoid in pregnancy'],['Assessment','Current seasonal and national guidance']],[PG,VS])
q('A patient returns late for the next dose in a routine multidose vaccine series. What is the usual principle?', 'Continue the series using the appropriate schedule rather than automatically restart because the interval was prolonged',[
 ('Every delay destroys all prior immune priming','That is not the usual immunization principle.'),('Give all remaining doses simultaneously regardless of minimum intervals','Minimum intervals still matter.'),('Declare the person permanently ineligible for vaccination','A delayed schedule can generally be resumed.')],
 'Longer-than-recommended intervals usually do not invalidate earlier valid doses', '**Review the product-specific catch-up schedule.**',['Doses given too early are a different problem.','Some products or exposure circumstances have special rules.'], '**Document valid doses and plan completion.**',['Use minimum intervals and age indications correctly.','Avoid unnecessary repeat doses from a missing-record assumption without assessment.'],[['Late dose','Usually resume'],['Too-early dose','Assess validity separately'],['Plan','Product-specific completion']],[VS])
q('A susceptible eligible contact is assessed soon after measles exposure. Which PEP timing is correct?', 'MMR within 72 hours when suitable, or immune globulin within six days for indicated contacts',[
 ('MMR at any time is equally effective for preventing the recent event','Timing affects post-exposure benefit.'),('Give live MMR routinely to a severely immunocompromised pregnant contact','Contraindications require a different strategy.'),('No preventive measure can help after exposure','Timely vaccination or IG can help in the appropriate setting.')],
 'Exposure prevention depends on both timing and eligibility', '**MMR and IG have different indications and windows.**',['Pregnancy, age and immune compromise affect selection.','Do not give both without considering the interference and schedule guidance.'], '**Coordinate public-health assessment promptly.**',['Assess evidence of immunity.','Provide monitoring and exclusion instructions according to the contact pathway.'],[['MMR','Within 72 hours when eligible'],['IG','Within six days when indicated'],['Selection','Immunity, age, pregnancy and immune status']],[MM])
q('A pregnant patient without varicella immunity has a significant exposure. Which prevention principle is appropriate?', 'Urgently assess for varicella-zoster immune globulin, given as soon as possible within the indicated window, rather than live varicella vaccine',[
 ('Give live varicella vaccine during pregnancy as routine PEP','It is contraindicated.'),('Wait for severe pneumonia before any assessment','Prevention must be considered promptly.'),('Recombinant zoster vaccine substitutes for varicella PEP','It does not provide that exposure-management strategy.')],
 'A high-risk susceptible contact may need passive rather than live-vaccine protection', '**CDC permits VariZIG within ten days of exposure when indicated.**',['Earlier administration is preferred.','Immunocompromised susceptible contacts can also qualify.'], '**Arrange follow-up for symptoms and prompt treatment if illness develops.**',['Immune globulin can alter the incubation/monitoring period.','Plan postpartum active vaccination when appropriate.'],[['Risk','Susceptible pregnancy'],['PEP','Prompt indicated VZIG'],['Live vaccine','Defer during pregnancy']],[VZ,PG],style='clinical_scenario')

